# Ledger data: sampled tool inputs (redacted, but local-only regardless).
*.jsonl
hook-errors.log
digest-latest.json
digest-prev.json
decided.json
.evaluate.lock
# Same-directory scratch of an atomic rename (record-decision.py writes
# `decided.json.tmp` then renames it over `decided.json`). It cannot move to
# ~/.local/state like the other runtime output here — rename(2) needs both
# names on one filesystem, in one directory — so this is the one case in this
# repo where an ignore rule IS the fix rather than a cover-up. Kept as a
# pattern, not `decided.json.tmp`: pinning the rule to the single filename
# that exists today is precisely the bug this change was opened to fix.
*.tmp
# run-evaluate.sh's per-process scratch (`.digest-latest.json.$$`,
# `.digest-prev.json.$$`, `.digest-latest.err.$$`, `.agent-raw.$$`). A trap on
# EXIT removes them, which covers every ordinary path and none of the ones
# that matter: SIGKILL, an OOM kill, a reboot mid-run. Same reasoning as
# *.tmp — the scratch has to sit beside its target for the atomic publish.
.digest-*.*
.agent-raw.*
# run-tests.sh builds its fake HOME at `$S/.testhome`, where $S is THIS
# directory — not `tests/`. The rule here read `tests/.testhome/` and so matched
# nothing; the suite clears the dir at setup rather than teardown (deliberately,
# so a failed run's state survives for inspection), which means it is always
# left behind and was always untracked-and-visible. Unrooted so it holds
# wherever the suite is run from.
.testhome/
__pycache__/
# Test-run artifacts (outputs, not sources)
tests/crontab.*
tests/pass*.out
tests/settings.after*.json
tests/fake-crontab.txt
tests/stage/
